  {
   "publisher": "Emerald Publishing Limited",
   "abstract": "<jats:p>Lesotho, like many other nations throughout Africa, is attempting to improve educational conditions for a wide variety of students. This chapter focuses on the inclusion of students with cognitive disabilities in regular primary schools. Interviews and observation data were collected and demonstrate that there are both successes and continued challenges to including such students in schools. In two schools studied, attitudes toward students were positive, and some teachers were successfully able to differentiate curriculum for students with disabilities. On the other hand, resource levels are low, and frontal teaching (which is generally ineffective for students with cognitive disabilities) appears be the dominant pedagogy in primary school classrooms.</jats:p>",
